                "entry_id": "cf40f5fb-a91c-3219-b59c-c95aa5897496",
                "word": "physicotheology",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "04db9913-b200-44a0-a1e3-f1fc14d1c59c",
                        "entry_id": "cf40f5fb-a91c-3219-b59c-c95aa5897496",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "physicotheologies"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "b740bcb6-2384-3151-8f65-1b4dfbd2ebfb",
                        "entry_id": "cf40f5fb-a91c-3219-b59c-c95aa5897496",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The view that evidence and sound arguments for God's existence can be derived from a study of the natural world; a study of the natural world intended to provide such evidence."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "countable",
                            "dated",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "cb66829e-15bf-4a7b-901a-fa20e6418c78",
                                "sense_id": "b740bcb6-2384-3151-8f65-1b4dfbd2ebfb",
                                "text": "What else can be concluded, but that all was made with magnificent Design, and that all the whole Structure is the Work of some intelligent Being; some Artist, of Power and Skill equivalent to such a Work?"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "859f89d7-7bf0-4acb-99f8-3529b9af29f0",
                                "sense_id": "b740bcb6-2384-3151-8f65-1b4dfbd2ebfb",
                                "text": "We have been rather severe in the foregoing observations on Dr. Büchner's work, because it belongs to a school of physico-theology, which, whether on the affirmative or negative side, we hold to be equally opposed to the true interests of science."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "d4a4919c-b0e9-4fcb-8e6d-8e9cbd6fea6a",
                                "sense_id": "b740bcb6-2384-3151-8f65-1b4dfbd2ebfb",
                                "text": "Indeed, Creation, like a number of passages in The Seasons, furnishes excellent examples of physicotheology, helpfully defined by Harry Solomon as \"the use of the scientific examination of nature to establish and raise admiration for the existence and providence of God.\""
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "931f134c-246c-41ea-b1af-c33d1b9d36d1",
                                "sense_id": "b740bcb6-2384-3151-8f65-1b4dfbd2ebfb",
                                "text": "Some claim that Kant's discussion amounts to a refutation of physicotheology and thus does not result in an argument from design."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
